{
  "id": "ch-multiint",
  "chapterNumber": "14",
  "title": {
    "en": "Multiple Integrals",
    "ar": null
  },
  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "A single integral accumulates over an interval; a <strong>double integral</strong> accumulates a function over a two-dimensional region — most concretely, the volume trapped under a surface \\( z=f(x,y) \\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Definition 14.1 — The double integral",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Partition a region \\( R \\) into small cells of area \\( \\Delta A \\), sample \\( f \\) in each, and sum. The <strong>double integral</strong> is the limit as the cells shrink:</p>\\[ \\iint_{R} f\\,dA=\\lim_{\\Delta A\\to 0}\\sum_{i,j} f(x_i,y_j)\\,\\Delta A. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Theorem 14.2 — Fubini's theorem",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Over a rectangle \\( R=[a,b]\\times[c,d] \\), a double integral equals an iterated integral, and the order may be swapped:</p>\\[ \\iint_{R} f\\,dA=\\int_a^b\\!\\!\\int_c^d f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx=\\int_c^d\\!\\!\\int_a^b f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "explorer",
        "title": {
          "en": "⊙ Explorer · Double Riemann sum",
          "ar": null
        },
